# Starter Questions & Answers, A–I. (topic, question, answer, sources)
# Original short answers written for Catholic My Pride; CCC = Catechism of the Catholic Church.
FAQ = [
('Abortion', 'What does the Catholic Church teach about abortion?',
 'The Church teaches that human life must be respected and protected absolutely from the moment of conception. From the first moment of his or her existence, a human being has the rights of a person, above all the right to life. Direct abortion, willed as an end or a means, is gravely contrary to the moral law. The Church also offers mercy, healing and forgiveness to all who regret an abortion, and support to mothers in difficulty.',
 'CCC 2270–2275; Jeremiah 1:5; Psalm 139:13'),
('Advent', 'What is Advent?',
 'Advent is the four-week season that begins the Church’s year. It prepares us for Christmas, when we remember Christ’s first coming at Bethlehem, and it turns our hearts to his second coming at the end of time. Its colour is violet, with rose on the third Sunday (Gaudete Sunday). It is a season of joyful, hopeful waiting, prayer and conversion.',
 'CCC 524; Isaiah 40:3; Mark 13:33'),
('Angels', 'Do angels really exist?',
 'Yes. The existence of angels, spiritual beings without bodies, is a truth of faith witnessed throughout Scripture and Tradition. They are personal and immortal creatures with intelligence and will, who glorify God and serve his plan of salvation. They are God’s messengers, as Gabriel was to Mary.',
 'CCC 328–336; Hebrews 1:14; Luke 1:26'),
('Angels', 'Does everyone have a guardian angel?',
 'The Church teaches that from infancy to death human life is surrounded by the watchful care and intercession of angels. Jesus said of little ones that “their angels in heaven always behold the face of my Father.” Catholics celebrate the Holy Guardian Angels on 2 October and often pray the “Angel of God” prayer.',
 'CCC 336; Matthew 18:10; Psalm 91:11'),
('Annulment', 'What is an annulment?',
 'An annulment (a declaration of nullity) is not a Catholic divorce. It is a finding by a Church tribunal, after careful study, that a valid marriage never existed because something essential was missing on the wedding day, such as free consent. A valid sacramental marriage, once consummated, cannot be dissolved by any human power.',
 'CCC 1629, 1640; Mark 10:9; Code of Canon Law 1095–1103'),
('Anointing of the Sick', 'What is the Anointing of the Sick?',
 'It is the sacrament by which the Church commends the seriously ill or elderly to the suffering and glorified Lord, that he may raise them up and save them. The priest anoints the forehead and hands with blessed oil. It gives strength, peace and courage, forgives sins if the person cannot confess, and sometimes restores bodily health. It is not only for the dying.',
 'CCC 1499–1532; James 5:14–15'),
('Apostles’ Creed', 'What is the Apostles’ Creed?',
 'The Apostles’ Creed is an ancient summary of the Christian faith, rightly considered a faithful summary of the apostles’ teaching. It is the baptismal creed of the Church of Rome and is prayed at the beginning of the Rosary. It professes faith in the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit, the Church, the forgiveness of sins and eternal life.',
 'CCC 185–197'),
('Apostolic Succession', 'What is apostolic succession?',
 'The apostles chose men to succeed them, handing on their authority by the laying on of hands. Bishops are the successors of the apostles in an unbroken line. Through them the Church keeps the faith and the sacraments that Christ entrusted to the apostles.',
 'CCC 77, 861–862; Acts 1:21–26; 2 Timothy 1:6'),
('Ash Wednesday', 'Why do Catholics receive ashes on Ash Wednesday?',
 'Ashes are a biblical sign of repentance and of our mortality. On Ash Wednesday, the first day of Lent, the priest marks our foreheads with ashes, saying “Repent, and believe in the Gospel” or “Remember that you are dust, and to dust you shall return.” It is a day of fasting and abstinence.',
 'Genesis 3:19; Joel 2:12–13; Jonah 3:6'),
('Assumption', 'What is the Assumption of Mary?',
 'The Assumption is the truth that at the end of her earthly life the Virgin Mary was taken up body and soul into heavenly glory. Pope Pius XII defined it as dogma in 1950. Mary shares in a unique way in her Son’s Resurrection, and her Assumption anticipates the resurrection of all Christians. It is celebrated on 15 August.',
 'CCC 966, 974'),
('Baptism', 'What does Baptism do?',
 'Baptism is the first sacrament and the gateway to the others. It frees us from original sin and all personal sin, makes us children of God and members of Christ and his Church, and gives us a share in Christ’s priesthood. It leaves a permanent spiritual mark, so it can never be repeated.',
 'CCC 1213, 1262–1274; John 3:5; Matthew 28:19'),
('Baptism', 'Why do Catholics baptise babies?',
 'Children are born with a fallen human nature and need the new birth of Baptism. The Church baptises infants because grace is a free gift that does not depend on our merit, and parents would deny a child a priceless gift by delaying it. Infant Baptism is an immemorial practice; Scripture tells of whole households being baptised.',
 'CCC 1250–1252; Acts 16:15, 33; Mark 10:14'),
('Baptism', 'Can anyone baptise in an emergency?',
 'Yes. Normally a bishop, priest or deacon baptises. In case of necessity, anyone, even a non-baptised person, can baptise, provided they intend to do what the Church does and pour water on the person’s head while saying: “I baptise you in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit.”',
 'CCC 1256'),
('Benediction', 'What is Benediction?',
 'Benediction is a service of adoration in which the consecrated Host is placed in a monstrance on the altar. The faithful pray, sing hymns such as “O Salutaris” and “Tantum Ergo”, and the priest or deacon blesses the people with the Blessed Sacrament, making the sign of the cross.',
 'CCC 1378–1379'),
('Bible', 'Why does the Catholic Bible have more books?',
 'The Catholic Old Testament has 46 books, including Tobit, Judith, 1 and 2 Maccabees, Wisdom, Sirach and Baruch. These were part of the Greek Old Testament used by the early Church and were recognised by Church councils long before the Reformation. Some Protestant Bibles later removed them. The Catholic Bible has 73 books in all.',
 'CCC 120, 138; Council of Trent'),
('Bible', 'How should Catholics read the Bible?',
 'Catholics read Scripture as the Word of God written by human authors under the inspiration of the Holy Spirit. We read it in the light of the whole Bible, the living Tradition of the Church and the harmony of the faith, paying attention to what the human authors intended. The Church urges all the faithful to read the Bible often.',
 'CCC 101–133; 2 Timothy 3:16; Dei Verbum 25'),
('Bishops', 'What does a bishop do?',
 'Bishops are successors of the apostles. Each diocesan bishop teaches, sanctifies and governs the local Church entrusted to him, in communion with the Pope and the other bishops. Bishops receive the fullness of the sacrament of Holy Orders and can ordain priests and deacons.',
 'CCC 874–896, 1555–1561'),
('Blessed Sacrament', 'Why is there a red lamp near the tabernacle?',
 'The sanctuary lamp burns near the tabernacle to show that Jesus is truly present in the Blessed Sacrament reserved there. That is why Catholics genuflect towards the tabernacle and keep a respectful silence in church.',
 'CCC 1379, 1183'),
('Catechism', 'What is the Catechism of the Catholic Church?',
 'The Catechism is an official, complete summary of Catholic teaching on faith and morals. Saint John Paul II published it in 1992. It has four parts: the Creed (what we believe), the Sacraments (how we celebrate), the Commandments (how we live) and Prayer (how we pray).',
 'Fidei Depositum (1992); CCC 11–17'),
('Celibacy', 'Why are Catholic priests celibate?',
 'In the Latin Church, priests are normally chosen from men who freely embrace celibacy “for the sake of the kingdom of heaven.” Celibacy is a sign of undivided devotion to Christ and to his people and points to the life of the world to come. It is a discipline, not a doctrine; Eastern Catholic Churches have married priests.',
 'CCC 1579–1580; Matthew 19:12; 1 Corinthians 7:32–35'),
('Commandments', 'What are the Ten Commandments?',
 '1. I am the Lord your God: you shall not have other gods. 2. You shall not take the name of the Lord in vain. 3. Keep holy the Lord’s Day. 4. Honour your father and mother. 5. You shall not kill. 6. You shall not commit adultery. 7. You shall not steal. 8. You shall not bear false witness. 9. You shall not covet your neighbour’s wife. 10. You shall not covet your neighbour’s goods.',
 'CCC 2052–2557; Exodus 20:1–17; Deuteronomy 5:6–21'),
('Communion of Saints', 'What is the Communion of Saints?',
 'It is the union of all the faithful: those on earth, those being purified in purgatory and the saints in heaven. We are one family in Christ, so we can pray for one another, and the saints in heaven intercede for us.',
 'CCC 946–962; Hebrews 12:1'),
('Confession', 'Why do Catholics confess their sins to a priest?',
 'Jesus gave the apostles the power to forgive sins: “Whose sins you forgive are forgiven them.” The priest acts in the person of Christ. In Confession we hear with our own ears that we are forgiven, receive grace to grow, and are reconciled with God and the Church, since sin also wounds the Church.',
 'CCC 1440–1445, 1461; John 20:22–23; James 5:16'),
('Confession', 'Can a priest tell anyone what I said in Confession?',
 'Never. Every priest who hears confessions is bound under very severe penalties to keep absolute secrecy about the sins confessed. This is called the sacramental seal, and it admits no exceptions, even to save his own life.',
 'CCC 1467; Code of Canon Law 983'),
('Confession', 'How do I go to Confession?',
 'Examine your conscience first. Then, with the priest: make the sign of the cross; say how long it has been since your last confession; confess your sins honestly, mortal sins by kind and number; listen to the priest’s advice and penance; pray an act of contrition; receive absolution. Afterwards, do your penance.',
 'CCC 1450–1460'),
('Confirmation', 'What is Confirmation?',
 'Confirmation completes the grace of Baptism. The bishop (or a priest) anoints the forehead with chrism, saying “Be sealed with the Gift of the Holy Spirit.” It roots us more deeply in God’s love, increases the gifts of the Holy Spirit and strengthens us to witness to Christ.',
 'CCC 1285–1321; Acts 8:14–17'),
('Conscience', 'Must I always follow my conscience?',
 'A person must always obey the certain judgement of his conscience. But conscience must be formed, by God’s Word, prayer, the teaching of the Church and good advice, because it can be mistaken. We have a duty to form our conscience well.',
 'CCC 1776–1802; Romans 2:14–16'),
('Creation', 'Can a Catholic believe in evolution?',
 'The Church has no objection to scientific study of how life developed, provided we hold that God is Creator of all, that the human soul is created directly by God, and that human beings are not the product of chance. Faith and reason cannot truly contradict each other, since both come from God.',
 'CCC 159, 279–301, 366; Humani Generis 36'),
('Cross', 'Why do Catholics make the sign of the cross?',
 'The sign of the cross is an ancient Christian prayer. By tracing the cross over ourselves “in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Spirit,” we profess the Trinity and the saving death of Jesus and ask for his protection. We begin and end prayers, and the day, with it.',
 'CCC 2157, 1235'),
('Crucifix', 'Why do Catholic crosses show Jesus on them?',
 'A crucifix shows the body of Christ to remind us how much he loved us. Saint Paul said, “We preach Christ crucified.” Catholics also rejoice in the Resurrection; the crucifix keeps before our eyes the price of our salvation.',
 '1 Corinthians 1:23; Galatians 6:14; CCC 618'),
('Deacons', 'What is a deacon?',
 'Deacons receive the sacrament of Holy Orders “not unto the priesthood but unto the ministry.” They proclaim the Gospel, preach, baptise, witness marriages, conduct funerals and serve the poor. Some are preparing for the priesthood (transitional deacons); others, who may be married, remain deacons for life (permanent deacons).',
 'CCC 1569–1571; Acts 6:1–6'),
('Death Penalty', 'What does the Church teach about the death penalty?',
 'In 2018 Pope Francis revised the Catechism to teach that the death penalty is inadmissible, because it is an attack on the inviolability and dignity of the person. The Church works for its abolition worldwide, while recognising the duty of the state to protect people by other effective means.',
 'CCC 2267 (2018)'),
('Devil', 'Does the devil exist?',
 'Yes. Satan is a fallen angel who freely and irrevocably rejected God. He tempts people, but his power is not infinite. He cannot prevent the building up of God’s Kingdom, and Christ has already conquered him by the Cross.',
 'CCC 391–395, 2851–2854; 1 Peter 5:8; 1 John 3:8'),
('Divine Mercy', 'What is the Divine Mercy devotion?',
 'It comes from the revelations of Jesus to Saint Faustina Kowalska in Poland in the 1930s. It includes the image of Jesus with the words “Jesus, I trust in you,” the Chaplet of Divine Mercy, prayer at three o’clock, the hour of his death, and Divine Mercy Sunday, the Sunday after Easter, established by Saint John Paul II in 2000.',
 'Diary of Saint Faustina; Psalm 136'),
('Doctors of the Church', 'What is a Doctor of the Church?',
 'It is a title the Pope gives to saints whose teaching has greatly helped the whole Church, such as Augustine, Thomas Aquinas, Teresa of Avila and Thérèse of Lisieux. “Doctor” comes from the Latin for teacher.',
 ''),
('Easter', 'Why is Easter the greatest Christian feast?',
 'Easter celebrates the Resurrection of Jesus, the crowning truth of our faith. By rising from the dead Christ conquered sin and death and opened eternal life to us. Every Sunday is a “little Easter”, and the Easter season lasts fifty days, until Pentecost.',
 'CCC 638–658, 1169; 1 Corinthians 15:14–20'),
('Ecumenism', 'What does the Church teach about other Christians?',
 'All who are baptised and believe in Christ are truly brothers and sisters in the Lord, though not in full communion with the Catholic Church. The Church prays and works for Christian unity, as Jesus prayed: “that they may all be one.”',
 'CCC 817–822, 838; John 17:21'),
('Eucharist', 'Do Catholics really believe the Eucharist is Jesus?',
 'Yes. In the Eucharist the Body and Blood, together with the soul and divinity, of our Lord Jesus Christ are truly, really and substantially present. At the consecration the bread and wine become the Body and Blood of Christ, though they still look and taste like bread and wine. Jesus said: “This is my body.”',
 'CCC 1373–1381; John 6:51–58; Luke 22:19–20'),
('Eucharist', 'How should I prepare to receive Holy Communion?',
 'A Catholic should be in a state of grace, without unconfessed mortal sin, and keep the Eucharistic fast of one hour from food and drink (water and medicine excepted). We approach with faith and reverence, answering “Amen” when the minister says “The Body of Christ.”',
 'CCC 1385–1388; 1 Corinthians 11:27–29; Code of Canon Law 919'),
('Euthanasia', 'What does the Church teach about euthanasia?',
 'Euthanasia, ending the life of sick, disabled or dying persons, is morally unacceptable. But it is not required to use extraordinary or burdensome treatments, and pain relief may be given even if it shortens life, as long as death is not intended. Palliative care is a special form of love.',
 'CCC 2276–2279'),
('Examination of Conscience', 'What is an examination of conscience?',
 'It is a prayerful review of our thoughts, words and actions in the light of God’s Word, especially the Ten Commandments and the Beatitudes, to recognise our sins before Confession. Many Catholics also make a brief examination every night.',
 'CCC 1454'),
('Faith', 'What is faith?',
 'Faith is both a gift of God and a free human act by which we believe God and everything he has revealed, because he is truth itself. It is a personal adherence to God and assent to what he reveals. Faith is necessary for salvation and must be lived out in love.',
 'CCC 142–184; Hebrews 11:1; James 2:17'),
('Fasting', 'What are the Church’s rules on fasting and abstinence?',
 'Ash Wednesday and Good Friday are days of fasting and abstinence. Fasting (ages 18–59) means one full meal and two small meals that together do not make a full meal. Abstinence (age 14 and up) means no meat. Fridays are days of penance, with abstinence or another penance as the local bishops decide.',
 'CCC 2043; Code of Canon Law 1249–1253'),
('Father', 'Why do Catholics call priests “Father”?',
 'Priests are spiritual fathers who bring people new life in Christ through the sacraments. Saint Paul wrote, “I became your father in Christ Jesus through the Gospel.” Jesus’ warning in Matthew 23:9 is against pride, not the word itself; Scripture often calls people “father”.',
 '1 Corinthians 4:15; 1 John 2:13; Acts 7:2'),
('Free Will', 'Did God give us free will?',
 'Yes. God created us free so that we could love him freely, since love cannot be forced. Freedom is the power to act or not to act, and to choose good. It reaches its perfection when directed to God; choosing evil is an abuse of freedom that leads to slavery to sin.',
 'CCC 1730–1748; Sirach 15:14; Galatians 5:1'),
('Genuflection', 'Why do Catholics genuflect?',
 'Bending the right knee to the floor is a sign of adoration before Jesus present in the Blessed Sacrament. “At the name of Jesus every knee should bend.”',
 'Philippians 2:10; CCC 1378'),
('God', 'Can we know God exists without the Bible?',
 'Yes. The Church teaches that God can be known with certainty from created things by the natural light of human reason. The order and beauty of the world and our longing for truth and goodness point to God. Revelation then tells us who God is and his plan of love.',
 'CCC 31–38; Romans 1:20; Wisdom 13:1–5'),
('Good Friday', 'Why is it called “Good” Friday?',
 'On Good Friday we remember the Passion and death of Jesus. It is “good” because by his death Christ saved us from sin and opened heaven. It is the only day of the year when Mass is not celebrated; instead there is the Celebration of the Lord’s Passion, with veneration of the cross.',
 'CCC 571–630; Isaiah 53:5'),
('Grace', 'What is grace?',
 'Grace is the free and undeserved help God gives us to respond to his call to become his children and share his divine life. Sanctifying grace is the gift of God’s life in the soul, received in Baptism. Actual graces are God’s help for particular moments.',
 'CCC 1996–2005; Ephesians 2:8'),
('Heaven', 'What is heaven?',
 'Heaven is perfect life with the Most Holy Trinity, the Virgin Mary, the angels and all the saints. It is the fulfilment of our deepest longings, the state of supreme and lasting happiness. In heaven we will see God “face to face.”',
 'CCC 1023–1029; 1 Corinthians 2:9; 1 Corinthians 13:12'),
('Hell', 'Does the Church teach that hell exists?',
 'Yes. Hell is the state of definitive self-exclusion from communion with God and the blessed. God predestines no one to hell; it is the result of freely choosing mortal sin and refusing God’s mercy until the end. The teaching is a call to responsibility and conversion.',
 'CCC 1033–1037; Matthew 25:41–46'),
('Holy Days', 'What are holy days of obligation?',
 'Besides every Sunday, Catholics must attend Mass on certain great feasts. The universal list includes Christmas, Mary Mother of God, Epiphany, Ascension, Corpus Christi, the Assumption, All Saints, the Immaculate Conception, Saint Joseph and Saints Peter and Paul, but each bishops’ conference may move or reduce them. Check with your local diocese.',
 'CCC 2177, 2180; Code of Canon Law 1246'),
('Holy Spirit', 'Who is the Holy Spirit?',
 'The Holy Spirit is the third Person of the Holy Trinity, truly God, equal to the Father and the Son. He is the Lord and giver of life, who spoke through the prophets, came upon the apostles at Pentecost and lives in the Church and in every baptised person, making us holy.',
 'CCC 683–747; John 14:26; Acts 2:1–4'),
('Holy Spirit', 'What are the gifts of the Holy Spirit?',
 'The seven gifts are wisdom, understanding, counsel, fortitude, knowledge, piety and fear of the Lord. They are permanent dispositions that make us docile to the Holy Spirit. They are strengthened at Confirmation.',
 'CCC 1830–1831; Isaiah 11:1–2'),
('Holy Spirit', 'What are the fruits of the Holy Spirit?',
 'The Church lists twelve fruits: charity, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, generosity, gentleness, faithfulness, modesty, self-control and chastity. They are signs that the Holy Spirit is at work in a person’s life.',
 'CCC 1832; Galatians 5:22–23'),
('Holy Water', 'What is holy water for?',
 'Holy water is water blessed by a priest. It is a sacramental. Blessing ourselves with it at the church door reminds us of our Baptism, and it is used in blessings of people, homes and objects. It does not work like magic; it disposes us to receive grace.',
 'CCC 1667–1668'),
('Icons', 'Do Catholics worship statues?',
 'No. Catholics worship God alone. Statues, icons and images are like family photographs: they remind us of Jesus, Mary and the saints and lift our minds to them. The honour given to an image passes to the person it represents. God himself commanded images of cherubim for the Ark.',
 'CCC 1159–1162, 2129–2132; Exodus 25:18–20'),
('Immaculate Conception', 'What is the Immaculate Conception?',
 'It is the truth that Mary, from the first moment of her conception, was preserved free from original sin by a singular grace of God, in view of the merits of Jesus Christ. It is not the conception of Jesus. Pope Pius IX defined it in 1854, and it is celebrated on 8 December.',
 'CCC 490–493; Luke 1:28'),
('Incarnation', 'What is the Incarnation?',
 'The Incarnation is the mystery that the eternal Son of God became man in the womb of the Virgin Mary by the power of the Holy Spirit. Jesus is one divine Person with two natures, truly God and truly man.',
 'CCC 456–483; John 1:14; Philippians 2:6–8'),
('Indulgences', 'What is an indulgence?',
 'An indulgence is a remission before God of the temporal punishment due to sins already forgiven. The Church grants it through the merits of Christ and the saints, under conditions such as Confession, Holy Communion, prayer for the Pope’s intentions and a set prayer or good work. It can be gained for oneself or for the dead. Indulgences are never sold.',
 'CCC 1471–1479'),
('Infallibility', 'Does infallibility mean the Pope can never sin?',
 'No. The Pope is a sinner who goes to Confession like everyone. Infallibility means that when the Pope, as supreme teacher, solemnly defines a doctrine of faith or morals for the whole Church, the Holy Spirit protects him from error. This is used rarely.',
 'CCC 889–891; Luke 22:32; Matthew 16:18'),
]
